  <SpecDocsLayout>
    <div class="pim-mapping-page">
    <h1 class="page-h1">Custom / in-house PIM mapping.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      The custom or in-house path applies to manufacturers without a commercial PIM. In the lighting industry this is typically sub-$50M brands, OEM/ODM suppliers, and family-owned specialty manufacturers. At the &gt;$100M tier (Signify, Acuity, Hubbell, GE Current, Louis Poulsen), the actual product master is typically SAP MM material master or a commercial PIM (Stibo, Pimcore): see the SAP and Salsify / Akeneo guides for those paths. This guide describes patterns that apply when the master is a custom relational database.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical in-house data model</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Products table</strong>: one row per SKU or family, with columns for identity, category, basic dimensions, rating.</li>
      <li><strong>Attributes table</strong> (typed columns or a key/value extension): for values that vary per product. Typed-column schemas (one column per attribute, strongly typed) are preferred where the attribute set is stable. EAV (entity-attribute-value) patterns are common in legacy lighting databases but introduce per-query joins and complicate type validation. ETIM's typed feature model (Alphanumeric, Numeric, Range, Logical) is the dominant industry approach for electrotechnical product attributes; consider it as the data-shape target whether or not your internal storage uses EAV.</li>
      <li><strong>Categories table</strong>: hierarchical or flat.</li>
      <li><strong>Files / assets table</strong>: cutsheet PDFs, IES, LDT; with a file-storage pointer (S3 key, SAN path) rather than the bytes.</li>
      <li><strong>Relationships table</strong>: accessory compatibility, replacement chains, kit composition.</li>
      <li><strong>Test reports table</strong> (sometimes): lab attestations with doc references.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Typical stacks vary by manufacturer vintage: 1990s-era industrial lighting brands often use IBM i (AS/400 / iSeries) with RPG or COBOL underneath, with newer web admins in Java or .NET. Mid-2000s-era brands typically use Oracle or PostgreSQL with Spring (.NET) or Django / Rails admins. The pseudocode in this guide is shown in Python for readability: adapt to whichever language your team owns.</p>

    <p>Sometimes the schema is fully normalized (one column per attribute); sometimes a "products + JSON blob" hybrid. Both work.</p>

    <h2>Core mapping patterns</h2>

    <h3>Identity</h3>
    <div class="pim-map-scroll">
    <table class="pim-map">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">SQL / ORM column</th><th scope="col">ULC path</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>manufacturer</code></td><td><code>product_family.manufacturer.slug</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Family / model-code column</td><td><code>product_family.catalog_model</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>SKU / order-code column</td><td><code>configuration.catalog_number</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>series</code></td><td><code>product_family.catalog_line</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Derived full slug</td><td><code>record_id</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Category</h3>
    <p>Maintain a mapping table from the in-house <code>category_id</code> to ULC enum values. Store as a version-controlled config file, not as a database table; ULC enums change with schema releases and should track the spec version. Example mapping:</p>
    <div class="pim-map-scroll">
    <table class="pim-map">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">In-house category</th><th scope="col"><code>primary_category</code></th><th scope="col"><code>mounting_types</code></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>recessed_downlight</code></td><td><code>downlight</code></td><td><code>["recessed_ceiling"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>linear_pendant</code></td><td><code>linear</code></td><td><code>["pendant"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>wallpack_exterior</code></td><td><code>bulkhead_wall_pack</code></td><td><code>["surface_wall"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>highbay_industrial</code></td><td><code>high_bay</code></td><td><code>["pendant", "surface_ceiling"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bollard_outdoor</code></td><td><code>bollard</code></td><td><code>["surface_floor"]</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h3>ETIM classification cross-walk</h3>
    <p>Many lighting product-master schemas in the European market reference ETIM class codes (for example, <code>EC002643</code> for downlights) and feature codes (for example, <code>EF001596</code> for housing material). ULC's <code>primary_category</code> enum and many of its individual fields cite ETIM feature identifiers inline in the schema descriptions where a direct correspondence exists. If your in-house PIM stores ETIM codes, use them as the bridge to ULC enums: the mapping is typically 1:1 at the class level and per-feature at the attribute level.</p>
    <p>ETIM North America is expanding adoption in US and Canadian distribution channels; ETIM UK and Continental Europe are well-established. See <code>mappings/etim-crosswalk.md</code> (planned in the upstream repo) for the full field-level table.</p>

    <h3>Dimensional fields</h3>
    <p>In-house PIMs usually store dimensions in a single authoritative unit. Convert at emit time to dual-unit. Common columns:</p>
    <div class="pim-map-scroll">
    <table class="pim-map">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">DB column</th><th scope="col">ULC path</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>overall_diameter_mm</code></td><td><code>product_family.physical_dimensions.overall_diameter</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>overall_length_mm</code></td><td><code>product_family.physical_dimensions.overall_length</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>recess_depth_mm</code></td><td><code>product_family.physical_dimensions.recess_depth</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>weight_kg</code></td><td><code>product_family.physical_dimensions.luminaire_mass</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Attestations</h3>
    <p>Common attestation programs the in-house schema should track: DLC QPL (DesignLights Consortium Qualified Products List, a lighting-specific energy attestation), ENERGY STAR Luminaires V2.0+, UL listings (UL 1598 and similar), and lumen-maintenance attestations (LM-80 / TM-21 derived L70 / L80 / L90 hours). The emitter maps each to a ULC <code>attestations[]</code> entry with <code>program</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>value_type</code>, and <code>verification</code> populated per the schema.</p>

    <h2>Gotchas</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Schemas without scenarios.</strong> If the PIM has one row per SKU without a scenario concept, synthesize scenarios from photometric data (one scenario per IES file). If there's only one IES per SKU, that's Pattern A (simplest).</li>
      <li><strong>Legacy free-text fields.</strong> In-house PIMs often have free-text fields mixing structured data ("90 CRI, 3000K, 120V"). Extract via regex during ETL, or flag for manual review.</li>
      <li><strong>Unit-of-measure inconsistency.</strong> Older schemas may mix metric and Imperial in the same column. Normalize during ETL.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing provenance history.</strong> If the PIM was hand-populated over years, default provenance to <code>{'{source: "manufacturer_direct", method: "transcribed"}'}</code> and plan a follow-up enrichment pass.</li>
      <li><strong>No change tracking.</strong> Without CDC, the emitter either runs full sweeps or polls <code>updated_at</code> columns.</li>
    </ol>

